The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
struct ip6t_opts {
    ...
    __u16 opts[IP6T_OPTS_OPTSNR]; // IP6T_OPTS_OPTSNR = 16
    ...
};
</text>
<text>
static int hbh_mt6_check(const struct xt_mtchk_param *par) {
    const struct ip6t_opts *optsinfo = par->matchinfo;
    if (optsinfo->invflags & ~IP6T_OPTS_INV_MASK) {
        return -EINVAL;
    }
    // Missing validation of optsnr field
    ...
}
</text>
<text>
for (i = 0; i < optsinfo->optsnr; i++) {
    if (optsinfo->opts[i] == ...)  // out-of-bounds access when optsnr > 16
        ...
}
</text>
Reason:
The `opts` array in `struct ip6t_opts` has a fixed size of 16 elements. The `hbh_mt6_check()` function validates other fields but does not check the `optsnr` value supplied from userspace. Consequently, a malicious or erroneous rule can set `optsnr` greater than 16. When the packet matching function (`hbh_mt6`) later iterates over `opts[0..optsnr-1]`, it accesses memory beyond the array bounds, leading to an out-of-bounds read (and the reported UBSAN crash).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
+	if (optsinfo->optsnr > IP6T_OPTS_OPTSNR) {
+		pr_debug("too many supported opts specified\n");
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
</text>
Reason:
This addition is placed inside `hbh_mt6_check()`, the rule setup path. By validating that `optsnr` does not exceed the maximum number of option descriptors (`IP6T_OPTS_OPTSNR`), the kernel rejects any rule with an oversized option list before it can be installed. This prevents the out-of-bounds array access during packet matching, following the common xtables pattern of performing input validation in `checkentry` (or `check`) functions.

